title: Lung cancer presentation and paraneoplastic syndromes
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Key decision:'' Recognize urgent local complications; paraneoplastic syndromes alone do not establish metastatic disease.
* ''Presentation:'' Persistent cough, hemoptysis, dyspnea, weight loss, or hoarseness warrant diagnostic evaluation.
** Common metastatic sites include brain, bone, liver, adrenal glands, and contralateral lung.
* ''Superior vena cava obstruction:'' Facial/arm swelling and distended neck/chest veins suggest venous obstruction.
** Obtain urgent assessment and tissue diagnosis when stable; tailor treatment to the cause.
** Airway compromise or cerebral edema requires emergency intervention; stenting can provide rapid relief.
* ''Squamous association:'' Parathyroid hormone-related peptide can cause hypercalcemia with suppressed native parathyroid hormone.
** Bone metastases are another cause; hypercalcemia alone does not distinguish these mechanisms.
* ''Adenocarcinoma association:'' Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy causes clubbing, painful periostitis, and joint symptoms.
* ''Small cell associations:'' Inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion causes euvolemic, hypo-osmolar hyponatremia.
** Ectopic adrenocorticotropic hormone causes hypokalemia, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and weakness; cushingoid appearance may be absent.
** Lambert–Eaton syndrome causes proximal weakness and autonomic symptoms through calcium-channel autoantibodies.
** Anti-Hu-associated sensory neuropathy or encephalomyelitis may precede the cancer diagnosis.
* ''Management:'' Treat the underlying malignancy and syndrome; seek early neurology input for neurologic presentations.

Sources: ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 11, pp. 532–533 (PDF 539–540); NCCN Small Cell Lung Cancer v1.2027, SCL-A 2, Discussion MS-4; [[NCI Cardiopulmonary PDQ, SVC syndrome|https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/cardiopulmonary-hp-pdq]].
